{
 "title": "How readable are Social Security's overpayment notice paragraphs?",
 "read_on": "2026-11-23",
 "standard": {
  "plain": "Social Security's own notice guide says to write at the sixth to eighth-grade reading level, with an average sentence of no more than 15-20 words, and to check the Flesch-Kincaid grade of each paragraph.",
  "source": "https://secure.ssa.gov/poms.nsf/lnx/0900610020"
 },
 "method": [
  "Each paragraph's text was read from the public POMS pages listed, in a browser, on the date shown, and scored there with tools/readability.js (the same file the tests run).",
  "Flesch-Kincaid grade = 0.39 x words per sentence + 11.8 x syllables per word - 15.59. Syllables are counted by a rule of thumb, so a grade can differ from Microsoft Word's by a few tenths.",
  "Fill-in blanks such as (1) are counted as one short word. Real filled-in notices may read a little harder or easier.",
  "Very short paragraphs (one sentence) give noisy grades; read the medians, not single values. Bullet lists joined by semicolons count as one long sentence, as Word would also count them.",
  "These are paragraph templates, not whole notices. A real notice is assembled from several of them, plus other text."
 ],
